Since there's no project file to confirm the target framework, here are the two lightest options — both avoid DI containers entirely:

---

### Option 1 – `TimeProvider` (.NET 8+, recommended)

.NET 8 ships a built-in `TimeProvider` abstract class. Accept it as an optional constructor parameter with a default:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _clock;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? clock = null)
        => _clock = clock ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _clock.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

(`FakeTimeProvider` is in the `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet package.)

---

### Option 2 – Ambient `IClock` (any .NET version)

Define a tiny interface + static default:

```csharp
public interface IClock { DateTimeOffset UtcNow { get; } }

public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly IClock _clock;
    public InvoiceCalculator(IClock? clock = null)
        => _clock = clock ?? SystemClock.Instance;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _clock.UtcNow > dueDate;
}

internal class SystemClock : IClock
{
    public static readonly SystemClock Instance = new();
    public DateTimeOffset UtcNow => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
}
```

Tests pass in a stub returning whatever time you need.

---

**TL;DR:** On .NET 8+, use `TimeProvider` — it's zero-dependency, one default parameter per class, and Microsoft ships a fake for testing. Want me to apply either of these to your `InvoiceCalculator`?
